<h2>The limitation architecture for Section 7</h2>
<p>An application under Section 7 IBC is governed by Article 137 of the Limitation Act — three years from when the right to apply accrues, ordinarily the date of default. The rigidity of that rule is tempered by Section 18: a written, signed acknowledgment of liability made before the limitation expires starts a fresh three-year period. The contested question of the last decade — whether entries in statutory balance sheets count — now has a settled, creditor-friendly answer, which this judgment consolidates:</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Authority</th><th>Holding</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Asset Reconstruction Co. v. Bishal Jaiswal, (2021) 6 SCC 366</td><td>Balance-sheet entries can constitute acknowledgment; caveats in directors&rsquo; reports must be read with them</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Dena Bank v. C. Shivakumar Reddy, (2021) 10 SCC 330</td><td>Section 18 not to be construed with pedantic rigidity in IBC; OTS offers within limitation can acknowledge a live claim</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Vidyasagar Prasad v. UCO Bank</td><td>Entries plus auditor&rsquo;s notes recording default on term loans are clear acknowledgment, even without naming the creditor</td></tr>
  <tr><td>IL&amp;FS v. Adhunik Meghalaya Steels, (2026) 3 SCC 794</td><td>Omission of the creditor&rsquo;s name in the balance sheet does not defeat acknowledgment where the jural relationship is otherwise evident</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Applying the law to the ledger</h2>
<p>The default of December 2013 put the primary three-year period to December 2016; the FY 2015-16 balance sheet — signed within that window — restarted the clock, and successive financial years&rsquo; entries carried it forward past the application&rsquo;s filing. The Court found the NCLAT had both misread the balance sheets and their appended notes, and decided without the benefit of the subsequent line of authority. The limitation holding is final; nothing was left open on it.</p>

<h2>The disciplined remand</h2>
<p>The judgment&rsquo;s procedural coda deserves attention. Before the NCLT the debtor had raised only two contentions — that the ARC was not a financial creditor, and limitation. The no-default argument surfaced for the first time in the Supreme Court. Rather than shutting it out entirely or reopening everything, the Court granted one opportunity, on the special facts, to argue default alone before the Adjudicating Authority. Parties resisting insolvency admission should note: pleas not taken before the NCLT are ordinarily lost, and the indulgence here was express and exceptional.</p>

<h2>Takeaways</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>For creditors and ARCs:</strong> harvest every acknowledgment — balance sheets, auditors&rsquo; notes, OTS correspondence — and plead them with dates in the Section 7 application itself.</p>
  <p><strong>For corporate debtors:</strong> balance-sheet hygiene has litigation consequences; entries made for accounting compliance will be read as acknowledgments unless accompanying caveats genuinely negate liability.</p>
  <p><strong>For assignees:</strong> an assignment does not break the acknowledgment chain — the debtor&rsquo;s acknowledgments enure to the assignee of the debt.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises a judgment of the Supreme Court of India for general information. It is not legal advice; limitation questions under the IBC turn on the documents in each case.</div>
